WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.020
The ribber.
Drop Stitch Lace technique can create

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:08.500
a beautiful lace-like fabric
without any stitch transfers.

3
00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.220
Imagine lace on a bulky machine.

4
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.860
The knitting is done mainly on the ribber.

5
00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:20.620
Then needles are selected on the main bed,
and this creates a lace texture.

6
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.120
Now, this is one of those techniques
that I think has been greatly ignored over

7
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:29.980
the years, and I believe it's
because it can be a bit confusing.

8
00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:35.300
We are used to working on the main bed,
and this technique is worked in reverse.

9
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.380
The knitting is on the ribber.

10
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.820
Before we get to the actual technique,

11
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.660
please take time to understand
how Drop-Stitch lace is created.

12
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.140
This video has two parts.

13
00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.580
First, we'll work the technique manually

14
00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.020
on the main bed,
putting the ribbing needles into work

15
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:58.820
manually to get a feel for how
this whole thing works.

16
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:03.220
Then we'll turn things around,
put the knitting on the ribber,

17
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:10.540
and let our main bed automatic
patterning select the needles for us.

18
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.490
Drop-Stitch lace is basically stockinette

19
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.740
with elongated stitches,
creating open spaces.

20
00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:23.290
It adds a pretty texture to both the knit
and the purl sides of the knitting.

21
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.700
I started by casting on a single
bed with a ribber comb.

22
00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:34.700
I just cast on full needle rib, hung
my comb, and dropped the ribber stitches.

23
00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:38.850
Now, this is a ribber technique,
and we're going to be dropping stitches.

24
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.820
So we need good even
weight on the knitting.

25
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:47.420
My carriage is on the right,
and I've raised my ribber.

26
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.020
The ribber carriage is connected.

27
00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:58.400
The ribber is set for full needle rib
racked to H, and the carriage is threaded.

28
00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.340
Just randomly,

29
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.100
I'm going to create a little design,

30
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:07.760
and I'm putting every fourth ribber
needle into working position.

31
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.980
And I knit to the left.

32
00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:20.000
Notice the loops over
those ribber needles.

33
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.890
Now I'm going to drop those ribber loops.

34
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:35.140
I'll disconnect my ribber carriage,
pass it across, drop the loops,

35
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:40.060
pass it back, and notice we've
got some loops hanging out here.

36
00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.780
We need to pull down to make
sure they're out of the way.

37
00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.500
We'll take any ribber needles out of work

38
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.600
at this point.

39
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:55.920
I'll knit one row to the right,
and that completes the first sequence.

40
00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.000
I'll put more ribber needles into work.

41
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.120
Knit left, create some loops
on the ribber needles.

42
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.060
Drop those loops.

43
00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:19.040
Take the ribber needles out of work,

44
00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.200
and knit back.

45
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.700
So you can see that the ribber
needles aren't actually knitting.

46
00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.500
They're not forming stitches.

47
00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:40.420
They're just gathering a loop
across every other row.

48
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:41.460
That's it.

49
00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:48.200
That's the idea behind Drop-Stitch lace.
But you can see that manually selecting

50
00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:52.640
the ribber needles isn't
the most practical idea.

51
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.640
Let's put our automatic
patterning into play.

